1
00:00:06,464 --> 00:00:07,716
[classical music plays]

2
00:00:09,426 --> 00:00:12,011
[Jerry] This is gonna be
a very special day today.

3
00:00:12,095 --> 00:00:16,433
Today we are driving
the 2004 Porsche Carrera GT.

4
00:00:19,978 --> 00:00:22,188
There are things,
there are special things.

5
00:00:22,897 --> 00:00:24,691
And then there is this thing.

6
00:00:29,404 --> 00:00:32,031
It was introduced to the public
in the year 2000

7
00:00:32,198 --> 00:00:34,951
on a rainy morning in Paris at 6:00 a.m.

8
00:00:39,122 --> 00:00:42,250
It was originally intended to be
a race car for Le Mans,

9
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:44,127
but they decided not to do the race

10
00:00:44,210 --> 00:00:47,797
and instead just kind of tame it down,
make it a streetcar.

11
00:00:48,047 --> 00:00:50,050
That was a good, crazy idea.

12
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:52,010
Credit for the design and execution

13
00:00:52,093 --> 00:00:55,555
goes to Porsche superstars
Grant Larson and Tony Hatter,

14
00:00:55,638 --> 00:00:57,182
one from US, the other UK.

15
00:00:57,932 --> 00:01:01,519
The similarity between great car design
and great comedy is in comedy,

16
00:01:01,603 --> 00:01:05,023
when you have a great joke, 
it has to be the exact perfect words,

17
00:01:05,106 --> 00:01:07,484
each one in the exact right place.

18
00:01:07,567 --> 00:01:09,569
I said, "What's the matter,
is there somebody else?"

19
00:01:09,652 --> 00:01:11,488
She looked at me.
She said, "There must be."

20
00:01:11,571 --> 00:01:13,198
[Jerry] Or it just doesn't quite work.

21
00:01:13,656 --> 00:01:15,700
In car design, every line and shape

22
00:01:15,784 --> 00:01:18,453
has to connect perfectly
with every other line and shape,

23
00:01:18,536 --> 00:01:20,497
or it just doesn't look quite right.

24
00:01:20,914 --> 00:01:23,500
This car looks quite right.

25
00:01:24,417 --> 00:01:27,420
Most cars, like, have some good angles,
some not good angles.

26
00:01:27,879 --> 00:01:30,256
This car, every way you look at it,

27
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:32,300
nothing cheesy, nothing juvenile,

28
00:01:32,383 --> 00:01:34,969
trying too hard, "Please, like me."

29
00:01:35,053 --> 00:01:38,181
It's just a perfect, mature, exciting,
beautiful shape.

30
00:01:39,224 --> 00:01:42,644
These cars cost a lot when they were new,
cost a lot to maintain.

31
00:01:42,727 --> 00:01:44,938
Is it worth it? Yeah, it's worth it!

32
00:01:45,021 --> 00:01:46,856
Life's a pain in the ass, too.
Is that worth it?

33
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:47,774
[revving]

34
00:01:47,857 --> 00:01:51,945
The engine is a normally aspirated V10.
Not eight, not twelve. Ten!

35
00:01:52,403 --> 00:01:53,863
But it's the sound of it.

36
00:01:53,947 --> 00:01:56,324
-[revving]
-This engine starts or revs,

37
00:01:56,407 --> 00:01:59,619
it's like ten mountain lions all clearing
their throat simultaneously...

38
00:01:59,702 --> 00:02:01,037
[revving]

39
00:02:01,121 --> 00:02:03,957
...and then going,
"All right. Let's go kill something."

40
00:02:04,290 --> 00:02:05,667
So many cool details.

41
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:08,002
The stick shift is made
of polished birchwood

42
00:02:08,086 --> 00:02:10,880
to resemble the legendary 917 race car.

43
00:02:10,964 --> 00:02:12,882
Why did they use birchwood?

44
00:02:13,591 --> 00:02:15,468
[singing voice] It's lighter.

45
00:02:16,594 --> 00:02:18,888
I need this car today 

46
00:02:18,972 --> 00:02:21,349
because it's one of the most special
Porsches of all time,

47
00:02:21,432 --> 00:02:25,186
and I am going to visit with one
of the most special comedians of all time.

48
00:02:25,270 --> 00:02:28,148
-Now, ladies and gentlemen...
-[Jerry] The legendary, spectacular,

49
00:02:28,231 --> 00:02:29,065
thrillingly funny...

50
00:02:29,149 --> 00:02:30,150
Eddie Murphy!

51
00:02:30,608 --> 00:02:31,734
-[bleep] you.
-[laughter]

52
00:02:32,068 --> 00:02:34,112
-[Jerry] Eddie Murphy and I both...
-Born in Brooklyn,

53
00:02:34,195 --> 00:02:36,573
-then move out the Long Island.
-You move out the Long Island.

54
00:02:36,656 --> 00:02:40,118
We started at the Comic Strip together
in Manhattan the exact same week,

55
00:02:40,201 --> 00:02:42,620
-July of 1976.
-[cheering]

56
00:02:42,704 --> 00:02:45,582
We've had very different career paths,
but still a lot in common.

57
00:02:46,249 --> 00:02:48,209
Here's a line up card
from a show Eddie and I did

58
00:02:48,293 --> 00:02:50,670
at a Chinese restaurant in 1979.

59
00:02:50,753 --> 00:02:52,547
Both of our names misspelled,

60
00:02:52,881 --> 00:02:53,715
and now look at us.

61
00:02:53,798 --> 00:02:57,218
Here we are, all grown up, a couple
of Long Island comedy gunslingers.

62
00:02:57,302 --> 00:02:59,304
-[laughing]
-I could not be more excited

63
00:02:59,387 --> 00:03:01,055
to be hanging out with him today.

64
00:03:01,139 --> 00:03:02,807
-Hey!
-[phone rings]

65
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:04,893
-[Jerry] Hey, man.
-[Eddie] 'Sup, man?

66
00:03:04,976 --> 00:03:06,853
[Jerry] Would you mind
if I use the bathroom?

67
00:03:06,936 --> 00:03:08,396
[Eddie] You got a take a one or two?

68
00:03:08,479 --> 00:03:11,065
[Jerry] Two? God, I don't like that.
That is horrible.

69
00:03:11,149 --> 00:03:11,983
[Eddie laughs]

70
00:03:15,069 --> 00:03:17,447
[Jerry] I think you should come along
for this one.

71
00:03:18,698 --> 00:03:22,285
On a very special
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

72
00:03:25,663 --> 00:03:27,582
Come on, pee. Do you really have to pee?

73
00:03:27,665 --> 00:03:28,499
[laughs]

74
00:03:28,833 --> 00:03:30,001
Who pees as a joke?

75
00:03:30,543 --> 00:03:33,087
-Wow. What is this? A Porsche?
-Yeah.

76
00:03:33,171 --> 00:03:34,672
-Carrera GT.
-Ah.

77
00:03:34,756 --> 00:03:36,883
This was one of their most exotic cars.

78
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:39,969
This car reminds me of you
because of the perfection.

79
00:03:40,053 --> 00:03:41,846
-Ah.
-It's got perfection.

80
00:03:41,930 --> 00:03:43,473
This car is together like you.

81
00:03:43,556 --> 00:03:44,807
Yeah, man. Thank you.

82
00:03:49,854 --> 00:03:51,940
-[Jerry] Have you had coffee yet today?
-[Eddie] No.

83
00:03:52,023 --> 00:03:52,941
Do you like coffee?

84
00:03:53,024 --> 00:03:54,525
-Yeah, I drink it everyday.
-Me too.

85
00:03:56,736 --> 00:03:58,821
You must've been making people laugh

86
00:03:59,656 --> 00:04:00,782
when you were a kid.

87
00:04:00,865 --> 00:04:03,201
You know, the first time I heard
a group of people laughing,

88
00:04:03,284 --> 00:04:04,619
I might have been, like...

89
00:04:04,994 --> 00:04:06,079
like eight or nine.

90
00:04:06,162 --> 00:04:07,372
We were on the bus...

91
00:04:08,414 --> 00:04:11,417
coming from a McCarren Pool in Brooklyn.

92
00:04:11,501 --> 00:04:13,294
And everybody that got off the bus,

93
00:04:13,544 --> 00:04:16,547
I was kind of, like,
doing like what they sound like,

94
00:04:16,798 --> 00:04:18,549
you know, and where they were going.

95
00:04:18,633 --> 00:04:20,760
And everybody on the bus
was kind of laughing.

96
00:04:20,843 --> 00:04:22,804
And they realized each time
they got off the bus,

97
00:04:22,887 --> 00:04:24,806
I was gonna be doing it about them,

98
00:04:24,889 --> 00:04:27,100
I realized
that the whole bus was laughing.

99
00:04:27,892 --> 00:04:30,478
-That's the first time it was a group.
-Right.

100
00:04:30,645 --> 00:04:33,064
And when I got off the bus, they clapped.

101
00:04:33,356 --> 00:04:34,649
-[Eddie chuckles]
-Right.

102
00:04:34,732 --> 00:04:37,110
And that's a big thing when you're a kid.

103
00:04:38,611 --> 00:04:40,863
But I still wasn't going,
"I'm gonna be a comedian."

104
00:04:40,947 --> 00:04:42,532
-No.
-I was just like,

105
00:04:42,615 --> 00:04:44,575
I knew I could make
a group of people laugh.

106
00:04:44,659 --> 00:04:46,160
I was once on a tour bus

107
00:04:46,244 --> 00:04:49,038
and the tour guide got off,
and I took the microphone,

108
00:04:49,122 --> 00:04:51,624
and I said,
"On your left, you have my right,

109
00:04:51,708 --> 00:04:54,252
and on your right, you'll have my left."

110
00:04:54,627 --> 00:04:55,795
[Eddie laughs]

111
00:04:57,005 --> 00:04:58,631
I was a kind of a loner, though.

112
00:04:58,715 --> 00:04:59,882
-I didn't have...
-Oh, really?

113
00:04:59,966 --> 00:05:02,051
...a group of people
that I hung out with, yeah.

114
00:05:02,135 --> 00:05:04,429
-Mostly, I was by myself.
-Wow!

115
00:05:04,929 --> 00:05:05,763
Me too.

116
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:09,517
Long Island's a funny place.

117
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:11,769
-There's nothing to do there but be funny.
-Yeah.

118
00:05:11,853 --> 00:05:14,897
Or accumulate experiences
that will one day be funny.

119
00:05:14,981 --> 00:05:17,608
I like Long Island. It was nice
when I was living out there.

120
00:05:17,692 --> 00:05:20,278
It was nice. Yeah, I liked it, too.

121
00:05:20,361 --> 00:05:23,698
Yeah, at the time I thought everyone
was a [bleep] idiot in Long Island.

122
00:05:23,781 --> 00:05:25,116
[both laugh]

123
00:05:29,537 --> 00:05:32,665
A manager that I had found
in the Yellow Pages in...

124
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:34,667
in Levittown.

125
00:05:34,751 --> 00:05:36,127
King Broder his name was.

126
00:05:36,210 --> 00:05:39,005
-Yeah. I remember that guy. Yes, right!
-King Broder.

127
00:05:39,088 --> 00:05:40,840
King Broder's actually
on Saturday Night Live.

128
00:05:40,923 --> 00:05:43,176
I used to do a character called Gumby.

129
00:05:43,259 --> 00:05:45,053
I remember Gumby.

130
00:05:45,136 --> 00:05:46,471
And he's kind of loosely based

131
00:05:46,554 --> 00:05:47,889
-on King Broder.
-Oh, yes.

132
00:05:47,972 --> 00:05:51,059
I can buy this network.
I'm a very rich piece of gum, baby.

133
00:05:51,142 --> 00:05:52,727
"Betty Rubble was a whore."

134
00:05:53,144 --> 00:05:55,396
You know, "I ain't know Betty Rubble."

135
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:56,981
[laughter]

136
00:05:58,149 --> 00:06:00,318
I never thought
I would be living in California.

137
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:02,445
-Really?
-Never.

138
00:06:02,528 --> 00:06:04,781
-'Cause you had a place in Jersey.
-Yeah, for years.

139
00:06:04,864 --> 00:06:07,700
I don't dislike it out here,
but I never thought I would live out here.

140
00:06:07,784 --> 00:06:09,118
And how's it going?

141
00:06:09,202 --> 00:06:10,745
-I'm cool with it. I like it.
-Yeah.

142
00:06:10,828 --> 00:06:12,205
I won't be missing stuff.

143
00:06:12,872 --> 00:06:14,040
It's kind of like, you know,

144
00:06:14,123 --> 00:06:16,084
-whatever's in front of me.
-Right.

145
00:06:16,501 --> 00:06:19,003
I don't do a lot of pining 
for the old days and...

146
00:06:19,087 --> 00:06:20,838
-Right.
-The good old days and that stuff.

147
00:06:20,922 --> 00:06:23,049
-That's pretty--
-Well, that happens, uh...

148
00:06:23,341 --> 00:06:24,342
It's kind of like..

149
00:06:24,425 --> 00:06:25,468
That's very good.

150
00:06:25,802 --> 00:06:29,222
A lot of people put a lot of work
into trying to get to that place.

151
00:06:29,305 --> 00:06:32,350
Right now is the best it's ever been.
And it only gets better.

152
00:06:32,433 --> 00:06:33,851
[upbeat music plays]

153
00:06:35,728 --> 00:06:38,314
Raw, your special from '87,

154
00:06:38,397 --> 00:06:40,441
it's so polished, it's so tight.

155
00:06:41,275 --> 00:06:44,028
-And...
-My suit that I was wearing?

156
00:06:44,112 --> 00:06:45,822
I can talk about the suit, too.

157
00:06:48,032 --> 00:06:50,451
-I told you that's my biggest regret is...
 -Yes.

158
00:06:50,535 --> 00:06:53,079
...stopping.
Like, I used to just go and do it.

159
00:06:53,454 --> 00:06:54,288
Right.

160
00:06:54,622 --> 00:06:56,582
Stopping and letting that muscle atrophy.

161
00:06:56,666 --> 00:06:58,501
-Yes. 
-It's my only regret.

162
00:06:58,584 --> 00:07:01,712
You don't like going up unless you really
have something on stage, you know?

163
00:07:01,796 --> 00:07:05,091
-I wouldn't go out on tour without an act.
-Right.

164
00:07:05,174 --> 00:07:07,718
The hardest thing about getting back
on stage again

165
00:07:07,802 --> 00:07:09,846
-is when you don't have anything.
-Right.

166
00:07:09,929 --> 00:07:12,265
I don't have any... anything.

167
00:07:12,348 --> 00:07:14,684
That's like a... a scary thought.

168
00:07:17,603 --> 00:07:21,232
Nobody could believe that you could be
on stage and not feel comfortable.

169
00:07:21,607 --> 00:07:22,608
But you can.

170
00:07:22,692 --> 00:07:24,360
-All comics are.
-Yeah.

171
00:07:25,111 --> 00:07:27,447
Even when you got it all the way down,
that's part of it.

172
00:07:27,530 --> 00:07:31,409
The times I've gotten on stage
and felt totally loose

173
00:07:31,492 --> 00:07:34,287
with no energy
of being uncomfortable at all,

174
00:07:34,370 --> 00:07:35,580
I-I would do shitty.

175
00:07:36,205 --> 00:07:38,833
-Yeah, I wouldn't have as strong a set.
-You wouldn't have?

176
00:07:38,916 --> 00:07:41,252
-No, that doesn't work if you--
-Right. Right.

177
00:07:41,335 --> 00:07:43,171
You gotta have a little bit of...

178
00:07:43,713 --> 00:07:45,548
-Yeah.
-"What if this shit doesn't...

179
00:07:45,631 --> 00:07:47,884
What if they don't get all of this shit?"

180
00:07:47,967 --> 00:07:50,511
I always do best
when I have a little bit of that.

181
00:07:52,305 --> 00:07:55,141
I love that I don't know what's there
till I walk out.

182
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:57,852
I don't know if I'm gonna feel strong
or weak or...

183
00:07:57,935 --> 00:07:59,312
Or how it's gonna be.

184
00:08:02,231 --> 00:08:06,068
Then it got different. It got to where
when I was working out stuff,

185
00:08:06,944 --> 00:08:08,946
it would be in the paper,
like, the next day.

186
00:08:09,030 --> 00:08:12,408
They were on me too much
during the creative process.

187
00:08:12,491 --> 00:08:15,828
I remember, uh,
Joan River's husband had died.

188
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:19,665
And I went up at the Comedy Store
and I did like some bit about, you know--

189
00:08:19,749 --> 00:08:22,168
'Cause she used to do all these jokes
about Edgar, "Oh, this--"

190
00:08:22,251 --> 00:08:24,545
-Yeah.
-He had committed suicide.

191
00:08:24,629 --> 00:08:27,590
And I did some joke like,
"He got tired of all them Edgar jokes."

192
00:08:27,673 --> 00:08:30,676
-Some shit I said that was...
-Right. [laughing]

193
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:33,638
It wasn't cool.
And it was in the paper the next day.

194
00:08:33,721 --> 00:08:35,431
And everyone was outraged and...

195
00:08:35,515 --> 00:08:36,515
-Right.
-Joan was hurt.

196
00:08:36,599 --> 00:08:38,643
And I was like,
"That wasn't even a bit yet."

197
00:08:38,726 --> 00:08:39,852
[upbeat music plays]

198
00:08:42,063 --> 00:08:43,814
When I first came to New York,

199
00:08:44,148 --> 00:08:46,067
-you and Larry Miller...
-Yeah.

200
00:08:46,150 --> 00:08:47,652
-...were the popular comics.
-Yes.

201
00:08:47,735 --> 00:08:50,446
And everybody was 
kind of like doing his cadence.

202
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:51,739
-Or your cadence.
-Right.

203
00:08:51,822 --> 00:08:55,076
You were the most polished,
and you were funnier than everybody.

204
00:08:55,159 --> 00:08:57,203
-And you were so smart.
-Oh, thank you. Thank you.

205
00:08:57,286 --> 00:08:59,372
Like, you didn't wanna follow you

206
00:08:59,914 --> 00:09:01,499
or George Wallace.

207
00:09:01,582 --> 00:09:03,209
-That's right.
-He was so big.

208
00:09:03,292 --> 00:09:05,461
"It's a crazy world!" I was like, "Shit."

209
00:09:05,545 --> 00:09:06,379
[chuckles]

210
00:09:06,462 --> 00:09:07,296
Man.

211
00:09:07,547 --> 00:09:09,757
-'Cause his presence was so big.
-Yes.

212
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:12,343
He's still big. And I'm still polished.

213
00:09:12,426 --> 00:09:13,469
[both laugh]

214
00:09:15,513 --> 00:09:20,268
You know that you not doing stand-up
drives people crazy. You know that, right?

215
00:09:20,351 --> 00:09:23,062
The last time I saw Don Rickles,

216
00:09:23,145 --> 00:09:25,815
and, uh... Rickles was, like,
maybe a month or two

217
00:09:25,898 --> 00:09:27,191
before he passed away,

218
00:09:27,275 --> 00:09:30,194
and he just went on and on and on
like that.

219
00:09:30,278 --> 00:09:32,530
-Like, "You have to do stand-up again.
-Really?

220
00:09:32,613 --> 00:09:35,116
You have to..."
Just went on and on about it.

221
00:09:35,199 --> 00:09:36,075
I was like, "Wow."

222
00:09:36,158 --> 00:09:37,910
-I'm gonna do it again. It was just--
-Really?

223
00:09:37,994 --> 00:09:39,161
Yeah, I just had to...

224
00:09:39,245 --> 00:09:40,746
You know, everything has to...

225
00:09:41,414 --> 00:09:42,248
be right.

226
00:09:42,915 --> 00:09:45,042
I gotta get up there
and start working out.

227
00:09:45,334 --> 00:09:46,168
Really?

228
00:09:46,586 --> 00:09:49,171
I have to get up and work...
The only way you can get, like, an act

229
00:09:49,255 --> 00:09:51,674
is I gotta go to clubs and work out.

230
00:09:52,091 --> 00:09:53,217
I'm gonna do that again.

231
00:09:53,301 --> 00:09:55,845
-You still gotta go to the comedy clubs.
-You still gotta go...

232
00:09:55,928 --> 00:09:57,179
You could have your own club.

233
00:09:57,263 --> 00:10:00,516
So you should buy the Comic Strip,
and I'll come work out there.

234
00:10:01,767 --> 00:10:03,561
If you wanna do that, I'll do it.

235
00:10:03,644 --> 00:10:06,063
I'll call it Jerry Seinfeld's Comic Strip.

236
00:10:08,608 --> 00:10:12,028
How did you go about
coming up with your stuff back then?

237
00:10:12,111 --> 00:10:14,613
Most stuff happens
when I'm talking to somebody,

238
00:10:14,905 --> 00:10:17,617
and I'll say something,
and it'll be, "Oh, that's funny."

239
00:10:17,700 --> 00:10:19,619
And I'll write down what I said.

240
00:10:19,702 --> 00:10:21,412
Usually, from conversations.

241
00:10:24,332 --> 00:10:25,416
You want some water?

242
00:10:26,375 --> 00:10:27,835
-You got water in here?
-No.

243
00:10:30,630 --> 00:10:32,590
-There's a lot of homeless out here.
-Yeah.

244
00:10:32,673 --> 00:10:34,050
I can't use this, but...

245
00:10:34,842 --> 00:10:36,636
whenever you see homeless,

246
00:10:37,219 --> 00:10:39,847
if you see more than one
and they're talking,

247
00:10:40,306 --> 00:10:41,390
you realize

248
00:10:42,141 --> 00:10:44,393
one of them is giving
the other one advice.

249
00:10:45,811 --> 00:10:46,896
[both laugh]

250
00:10:48,898 --> 00:10:50,274
-You can use that.
-You can?

251
00:10:50,358 --> 00:10:51,651
Yeah, you can say that.

252
00:10:52,151 --> 00:10:54,445
What, you thought that'd be
too hard on the homeless?

253
00:10:54,528 --> 00:10:55,988
-Yeah.
-Nah, that's a very funny joke.

254
00:10:56,072 --> 00:10:57,948
'Cause any time two people talk,

255
00:10:58,032 --> 00:11:00,618
one person's telling the other one
what to do. Right?

256
00:11:00,701 --> 00:11:02,078
-Absolutely.
-[Jerry laughs]

257
00:11:02,161 --> 00:11:03,496
[Eddie laughs]

258
00:11:04,288 --> 00:11:06,791
I'll tell you what you should do.
Here's what I've learned.

259
00:11:08,876 --> 00:11:12,171
I think it's funny when someone
does a really bad movie

260
00:11:12,546 --> 00:11:15,049
and then the poster's still up
for a few weeks.

261
00:11:15,549 --> 00:11:16,592
[both laugh]

262
00:11:16,675 --> 00:11:18,761
Especially if you're smiling
in your poster,

263
00:11:18,844 --> 00:11:20,554
if you're like this in the poster...

264
00:11:22,264 --> 00:11:25,851
The movie came out, didn't do shit,
the poster still be up like...

265
00:11:26,602 --> 00:11:27,478
[Eddie laughs]

266
00:11:28,354 --> 00:11:30,523
And on television, the worst is, uh...

267
00:11:30,898 --> 00:11:31,941
dance... dancing.

268
00:11:32,024 --> 00:11:33,943
If you've got a show, whatever it is,

269
00:11:34,026 --> 00:11:38,072
if you do anything like this...  Dang!
They'll be like this, "This Wednesday..."

270
00:11:39,073 --> 00:11:41,617
-They love to get moving and dancing.
-Yeah.

271
00:11:41,701 --> 00:11:43,953
If you're dancing
on the bottom of the screen,

272
00:11:44,203 --> 00:11:45,538
it's a bad show.

273
00:11:49,250 --> 00:11:51,460
What do you do first thing
in the morning?

274
00:11:51,544 --> 00:11:52,503
I, uh...

275
00:11:53,379 --> 00:11:54,630
eat some, uh...

276
00:11:55,506 --> 00:11:56,340
prunes...

277
00:11:56,424 --> 00:11:58,509
Prunes and raisin bran.

278
00:11:58,801 --> 00:12:00,261
Prunes and raisin bran?

279
00:12:00,678 --> 00:12:03,013
Well, I know
what the rest of your day's like.

280
00:12:04,348 --> 00:12:05,641
I'm totally-- I'm lazy.

281
00:12:05,725 --> 00:12:08,561
I like, more than anything,
I like to be doing nothing

282
00:12:08,894 --> 00:12:10,604
with my family and my kids.

283
00:12:10,688 --> 00:12:12,732
-Oh, can you sit still now?
-Absolutely.

284
00:12:12,815 --> 00:12:14,483
-Really?
-That's what I do mostly.

285
00:12:14,567 --> 00:12:16,360
-Really?
-That's what I do mostly,

286
00:12:16,444 --> 00:12:17,653
is just do nothing.

287
00:12:17,987 --> 00:12:19,905
Doing nothing is my favorite dish.

288
00:12:19,989 --> 00:12:21,866
And I mean, really nothing, like, uh,

289
00:12:21,949 --> 00:12:25,703
don't watch the news and don't read...
Don't know who's... what's going on. 

290
00:12:25,786 --> 00:12:28,247
-Right.
-Totally, like, disconnect.

291
00:12:30,958 --> 00:12:34,295
When you go out, do you ever think about,
"I can't wear these shoes

292
00:12:34,378 --> 00:12:36,005
'cause I might get into a fight.

293
00:12:36,088 --> 00:12:38,716
I want traction, I don't wanna be able
to slip around.

294
00:12:39,467 --> 00:12:41,594
I'm ready, I'm always ready. I'm ready.

295
00:12:41,927 --> 00:12:43,929
Just want you [bleep] to know I'm ready.

296
00:12:44,013 --> 00:12:45,306
[Jerry laughs]

297
00:12:45,556 --> 00:12:48,559
You know, if it comes to that,
I'm ready for that.

298
00:12:48,726 --> 00:12:51,353
I still have it within me to go there."

299
00:12:51,437 --> 00:12:52,521
[Jerry laughs]

300
00:12:52,605 --> 00:12:53,564
[Eddie laughs]

301
00:13:05,284 --> 00:13:06,577
[Jerry laughs]

302
00:13:21,717 --> 00:13:23,302
I am so happy to see you.

303
00:13:23,385 --> 00:13:25,221
I... I love you so much.

304
00:13:25,304 --> 00:13:27,807
And always have. Always have.

305
00:13:28,182 --> 00:13:29,099
Thanks.

306
00:13:29,475 --> 00:13:30,309
Yeah, man.

307
00:13:30,392 --> 00:13:33,020
You've just always been
the magical guy to me.

308
00:13:33,103 --> 00:13:34,522
-You would just--
-I told you,

309
00:13:34,605 --> 00:13:36,524
you were always the funniest.

310
00:13:36,607 --> 00:13:38,692
-Oh, thank you, please.
-You were always the funniest.

311
00:13:38,776 --> 00:13:41,320
When I started out,
you were the funniest one.

312
00:13:41,403 --> 00:13:42,446
-Always.
-Hello.

313
00:13:42,530 --> 00:13:43,364
Still.

314
00:13:46,367 --> 00:13:49,161
Do you remember The Big Laugh Off of '78?

315
00:13:49,245 --> 00:13:50,496
I was in The Big Laugh Off.

316
00:13:51,872 --> 00:13:55,626
See, the South Queens basketball team
suddenly become undefeated.

317
00:13:55,709 --> 00:13:57,044
[laughter]

318
00:13:57,461 --> 00:13:58,504
[applause]

319
00:13:58,587 --> 00:14:00,214
I hope you had fun. I gotta go home.

320
00:14:00,297 --> 00:14:01,382
I was there.

321
00:14:01,465 --> 00:14:04,593
That's where I met Robert Klein.
That night.

322
00:14:04,885 --> 00:14:07,847
'Cause I have that picture with him,
and I know it was at the Copa.

323
00:14:07,930 --> 00:14:10,432
It's so cool to be able to say
you played the Copa.

324
00:14:10,516 --> 00:14:13,018
-Yeah. Yeah.
-It just sounds really show business-y.

325
00:14:13,102 --> 00:14:14,603
It's so cruel, these shows,

326
00:14:14,687 --> 00:14:17,147
where they make people think,
"You've got it!"

327
00:14:17,481 --> 00:14:20,109
You win the contest and you think,
"Okay, I won.

328
00:14:20,526 --> 00:14:23,821
They have allowed me
into the entertainment industry."

329
00:14:24,363 --> 00:14:25,364
No they haven't.

330
00:14:25,698 --> 00:14:27,449
Yeah, you did well tonight, that's it.

331
00:14:27,533 --> 00:14:28,617
[jazz plays]

332
00:14:30,202 --> 00:14:33,205
-[Jerry] And what do watch?
-Have you ever watched the... [laughs]

333
00:14:33,289 --> 00:14:37,376
the little women, the little midgets...
the little people show?

334
00:14:37,626 --> 00:14:39,211
I have not, but I've heard about it.

335
00:14:39,712 --> 00:14:42,882
If you discriminate against them,
they're a minority.

336
00:14:42,965 --> 00:14:45,509
-Yeah. You can't use the word "midget."
-I know that.

337
00:14:45,885 --> 00:14:49,847
But I said, "Was there a meeting to decide
what are we gonna be called, then?

338
00:14:50,139 --> 00:14:53,183
Did they have any other... names?"

339
00:14:53,559 --> 00:14:56,729
-Uh, "petite-eo."
-No. You can't say "petite-eo."

340
00:14:56,812 --> 00:14:58,439
-[laughs]
-You can't say no shit like that.

341
00:14:58,522 --> 00:15:00,399
-Nothing... none of that?
-Gotta get rid of that.

342
00:15:00,482 --> 00:15:02,693
They'll be like,
"That's the same as saying "nigger.'"

343
00:15:06,614 --> 00:15:09,491
Who did you look at
and went, "Oh, my God!"?

344
00:15:09,575 --> 00:15:13,037
Richard was the first person that made me
think about being a comedian.

345
00:15:13,120 --> 00:15:14,997
But Richard was the first one where...

346
00:15:15,080 --> 00:15:18,626
It was an album that came out in '75
called That Nigger's Crazy.

347
00:15:18,709 --> 00:15:20,127
-Right.
-That album...

348
00:15:21,128 --> 00:15:25,716
I remember getting it and listening to it
over and over and over

349
00:15:25,799 --> 00:15:28,260
-and over and over, like, by myself.
-Yeah.

350
00:15:28,344 --> 00:15:30,930
-And all the time, just over and over.
-Yeah, yeah.

351
00:15:31,013 --> 00:15:33,766
I was like that with Cosby's
Why Is There Air?

352
00:15:33,849 --> 00:15:36,352
Why Is There Air? drove me crazy.

353
00:15:36,435 --> 00:15:37,811
I wore it out.

354
00:15:40,314 --> 00:15:43,025
Where's the first place
you remember standing on a stage?

355
00:15:43,108 --> 00:15:45,819
At the youth center.
They had a talent contest.

356
00:15:46,278 --> 00:15:48,864
I had a tape recorder, and I taped it.

357
00:15:49,073 --> 00:15:51,367
And afterwards,
when I was listening to it,

358
00:15:51,867 --> 00:15:54,870
it blew my mind 'cause it sounded
like one of the Richard albums.

359
00:15:54,954 --> 00:15:57,414
Like, an album... It was like,
you could hear people laughing.

360
00:15:57,498 --> 00:15:59,041
-Right.
-You could hear glass broke,

361
00:15:59,124 --> 00:16:01,335
like somebody knocked something over.
Could hear all that.

362
00:16:01,418 --> 00:16:03,754
-Oh, my God.
-And I was in from then.

363
00:16:04,088 --> 00:16:06,173
You realize your memory is amazing?

364
00:16:06,674 --> 00:16:07,925
I have a really good memory.

365
00:16:08,008 --> 00:16:11,136
You remember the name of the Chinese
restaurant gig we did in Jersey?

366
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:12,137
The Jade Fountain.

367
00:16:13,013 --> 00:16:14,932
-The Jade Fountain.
-That's crazy.

368
00:16:15,015 --> 00:16:16,976
The Jade Fountain.
Do you remember that place?

369
00:16:17,059 --> 00:16:18,602
-Yeah, vaguely.
-And the owner would say,

370
00:16:18,686 --> 00:16:21,563
"Hey, let me put your picture up here.
I'll give you exposure.

371
00:16:21,647 --> 00:16:24,400
Give me one of your pictures,
you'll get a lot of exposure."

372
00:16:24,483 --> 00:16:27,695
Exposure in the lounge
of the Jade Fountain.

373
00:16:31,198 --> 00:16:32,658
Okay, you're listening to the albums,

374
00:16:32,741 --> 00:16:35,744
you're making your friends laugh,
you're building, like, a bridge.

375
00:16:35,828 --> 00:16:38,706
It's like two cliffs to me, right?

376
00:16:38,789 --> 00:16:40,416
You're building this bridge
from each side.

377
00:16:40,499 --> 00:16:43,210
You keep listening, listening
to the album. "That bit is so funny."

378
00:16:43,293 --> 00:16:45,087
It makes you laugh. 
You can't believe this guy

379
00:16:45,170 --> 00:16:46,922
can make you laugh over and over again.

380
00:16:47,006 --> 00:16:50,300
And then you're making your friends laugh,
and then they meet.

381
00:16:50,592 --> 00:16:54,763
And you get up on a stage and make people
that don't even know who you are laugh.

382
00:16:54,847 --> 00:16:57,141
When I passed the audition
at the Comic Strip,

383
00:16:57,224 --> 00:16:58,726
I had been working out on the island.

384
00:16:58,809 --> 00:17:00,102
You gotta get off the island.

385
00:17:00,185 --> 00:17:02,896
When I passed,
I was bragging to everybody,

386
00:17:02,980 --> 00:17:05,858
"You gotta pay five dollars
to hear me now."

387
00:17:05,941 --> 00:17:08,736
The cover charge was five dollars
at the Comic Strip.

388
00:17:08,819 --> 00:17:10,529
-Even though you wasn't getting paid?
-Yeah.

389
00:17:10,612 --> 00:17:12,698
"So, you gotta pay five dollars
to get me."

390
00:17:12,781 --> 00:17:14,491
-Yeah. [laughing]
-"I get nothing.

391
00:17:15,159 --> 00:17:17,327
-But you gotta pay to see me."
-Yeah.

392
00:17:19,913 --> 00:17:21,457
The place was Catch.

393
00:17:21,665 --> 00:17:23,459
-Catch.
-That was the room you wanted to be in.

394
00:17:23,542 --> 00:17:24,835
That... that was the place.

395
00:17:24,918 --> 00:17:27,463
-Freddie Prinze and all those guys.
-Yeah. Rodney and Brenner. 

396
00:17:27,546 --> 00:17:30,174
-That's where they went.
-Yeah, so you really felt like,

397
00:17:30,257 --> 00:17:31,383
that room really had that.

398
00:17:31,467 --> 00:17:33,594
Like, look who had been discovered there.

399
00:17:35,721 --> 00:17:38,348
The most fun I've ever had working
on a movie was with John Landis.

400
00:17:38,432 --> 00:17:40,517
He's funny, and he directs you.

401
00:17:40,601 --> 00:17:41,935
Like, he actually was saying,

402
00:17:42,019 --> 00:17:44,563
-"Say it like this, and do this..."
-Right.

403
00:17:44,646 --> 00:17:47,691
See, you can say that to a comic.
You can't say that to an actor.

404
00:17:47,775 --> 00:17:49,568
-What's that?
-"Say it like this."

405
00:17:49,651 --> 00:17:51,570
-Yeah, he would say that to--
-Comics don't care.

406
00:17:51,653 --> 00:17:53,197
-Just tell me how to say it.
-Yeah.

407
00:17:55,240 --> 00:17:57,576
Then it started feeling more like work.

408
00:17:57,659 --> 00:17:59,870
-Well, because the money gets bigger.
-Yeah--

409
00:17:59,953 --> 00:18:02,664
And the fun gets smaller.
That's the problem.

410
00:18:02,748 --> 00:18:06,085
Comedy and money, to me,
are antithetical.

411
00:18:06,168 --> 00:18:09,505
'Cause one of the things that makes things
funny is when you don't have any money.

412
00:18:09,838 --> 00:18:12,466
I feel like when you add money
to a project,

413
00:18:12,674 --> 00:18:14,802
you're tampering with the comedy.

414
00:18:14,885 --> 00:18:17,346
'Cause all of a sudden,
we're building big things,

415
00:18:17,971 --> 00:18:20,682
taking time... taking the fun out of it.

416
00:18:20,766 --> 00:18:22,434
And with a little pressure.

417
00:18:22,518 --> 00:18:25,395
Yeah. Little pressure, little cheap,
little sloppy.

418
00:18:25,771 --> 00:18:28,565
Pressure. Like I said,
when you get ready to go on stage,

419
00:18:28,649 --> 00:18:30,734
-and you just feel like--
-Right, that little edge.

420
00:18:30,818 --> 00:18:32,361
-You gotta... That's crucial.
-Yeah.

421
00:18:35,364 --> 00:18:38,200
The worst I ever did, ever,
I did a jazz club

422
00:18:38,283 --> 00:18:39,952
out on... in Queens.

423
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:42,162
And my Dad had to drive me to the gig.

424
00:18:42,663 --> 00:18:44,373
And I bombed horribly.

425
00:18:44,456 --> 00:18:47,668
And I was supposed to have enough money
to get a cab back home,

426
00:18:47,751 --> 00:18:50,879
and I did so bad that the guy
wouldn't pay me.

427
00:18:51,296 --> 00:18:52,714
-[laughs]
-Oh, my God.

428
00:18:53,215 --> 00:18:54,133
Gerald's.

429
00:18:54,716 --> 00:18:57,344
-I was supposed to get 50 bucks, right?
-Oh, my God. Right.

430
00:18:57,427 --> 00:18:59,721
So, I was supposed to do two shows.
I go on, he says,

431
00:18:59,805 --> 00:19:02,349
"Yeah, you gonna do one set,
and then, uh...

432
00:19:02,432 --> 00:19:04,017
then the band's gonna come on.

433
00:19:04,101 --> 00:19:05,310
Then you have a break.

434
00:19:05,394 --> 00:19:08,021
Then, you know, you come on again."
I was like, "Okay."

435
00:19:08,105 --> 00:19:09,439
So, I go on,

436
00:19:09,940 --> 00:19:10,858
and I was so bad.

437
00:19:10,941 --> 00:19:12,234
And there was no dressing room.

438
00:19:12,317 --> 00:19:15,904
So I bombed, then afterwards,
you had to go sit in the room

439
00:19:15,988 --> 00:19:18,073
with the people you just bombed with.
[laughs]

440
00:19:20,826 --> 00:19:23,912
You're sitting there, people are
walking by going, "Oh, shit, dude."

441
00:19:24,788 --> 00:19:26,206
I'm, like, 17.

442
00:19:26,290 --> 00:19:27,332
[Jerry laughs]

443
00:19:27,416 --> 00:19:29,543
Then they'd say, "We're gonna bring
that comedian back."

444
00:19:29,626 --> 00:19:32,337
And you would hear people go, "Oh, shit!"

445
00:19:34,590 --> 00:19:37,050
And then, afterwards,
I'd go up to Gerald...

446
00:19:37,426 --> 00:19:38,719
He was a grown man, too.

447
00:19:38,802 --> 00:19:40,721
It was really mean to be like this to me.

448
00:19:40,804 --> 00:19:44,224
I went up, I was like, "Yes, I was
supposed to get $50 for the night."

449
00:19:44,308 --> 00:19:47,477
He turned around and said, "You better
get the [bleep] out of my face."

450
00:19:47,561 --> 00:19:48,645
[both laugh]

451
00:19:51,148 --> 00:19:55,736
Then my father had to drive out to Queens
at two in the morning and drive me back.

452
00:19:55,819 --> 00:19:59,197
"Oh, you a comedian, huh? Comedian...
Now, you gonna get you a job! 

453
00:19:59,281 --> 00:20:01,074
And you gonna get out
at nine in the morning,

454
00:20:01,158 --> 00:20:03,702
and you come back after five,
[bleep] comedian!

455
00:20:03,785 --> 00:20:05,370
Comedian my ass!"

456
00:20:10,125 --> 00:20:12,294
-I'm a careful man.
-Yes, you are.

457
00:20:12,669 --> 00:20:13,921
But not in your comedy.

458
00:20:14,004 --> 00:20:17,132
You were definitely a fearless comedian.

459
00:20:21,720 --> 00:20:23,680
I wouldn't say I was fearless, though.

460
00:20:24,056 --> 00:20:26,266
I wasn't just I'd just say anything,
though.

461
00:20:26,683 --> 00:20:28,352
You too. You were really confident.

462
00:20:28,435 --> 00:20:30,979
-Not like you.
-That's what you had... I'm telling you.

463
00:20:31,063 --> 00:20:35,025
You were the first comic
that seemed like you knew it.

464
00:20:35,108 --> 00:20:37,277
Like, you got it 
and you seemed like you had--

465
00:20:37,361 --> 00:20:40,280
Well, if I did, that was just me
acting  like that,

466
00:20:40,364 --> 00:20:43,408
'cause I know that's what works.
But to me, when I watched you...

467
00:20:45,160 --> 00:20:48,372
I thought,
"This guy's not scared of anything."

468
00:20:48,455 --> 00:20:49,957
Nothing is more fun...

469
00:20:50,624 --> 00:20:52,751
-than seeing someone you know bomb...
-Yes, yes.

470
00:20:52,834 --> 00:20:53,669
[laughs]

471
00:20:53,752 --> 00:20:56,338
And them knowing you're in the audience
and watching them.

472
00:20:56,421 --> 00:20:57,297
And it also...

473
00:20:57,381 --> 00:21:00,968
it makes the experience more fun
for the comic that's... that's on stage.

474
00:21:01,051 --> 00:21:02,552
At any time I'm dying,

475
00:21:03,095 --> 00:21:06,473
and it's somebody in the back,
that knowing laugh,

476
00:21:06,556 --> 00:21:08,767
-that always makes it easier to bomb.
-Right.

477
00:21:10,018 --> 00:21:12,396
When was the last time
you had to use Raid?

478
