00:31:38,730 --> 00:31:40,690
-Wow.
-And I was such an Ape maniac,

718
00:31:41,107 --> 00:31:44,235
that I remember that they had
"Go Ape for a Day,"

719
00:31:44,444 --> 00:31:46,529
in, uh, Hempstead, Long Island.

720
00:31:46,613 --> 00:31:49,949
It was like they were gonna show 
all five Ape movies back to back.

721
00:31:50,283 --> 00:31:53,661
And, uh, I went by myself,
in the... in the morning,

722
00:31:53,745 --> 00:31:56,706
-and I watched all those movies twice.
-[Jerry laughs]

723
00:31:56,789 --> 00:31:58,917
-Twice. I sat through...
-Wow!

724
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:00,418
...ten Ape movies,

725
00:32:00,710 --> 00:32:02,879
and when I got out of the movies,
it was dark.

726
00:32:02,962 --> 00:32:04,797
And I went home, and my mother gave me...

727
00:32:04,881 --> 00:32:08,968
My last whippin' was because
I went on some Ape movie marathon.

728
00:32:10,511 --> 00:32:12,722
So, there was Escape, there was Beneath...

729
00:32:12,805 --> 00:32:14,223
The first is Planet of the Apes.

730
00:32:14,307 --> 00:32:15,808
-Planet, Beneath.
-Then it's Beneath.

731
00:32:15,892 --> 00:32:17,727
-Then it's Escape.
-Then it's Escape.

732
00:32:17,810 --> 00:32:19,479
-Then it's Conquest.
-Then it's Conquest. 

733
00:32:19,562 --> 00:32:20,396
Then it's Battle.

734
00:32:20,480 --> 00:32:21,731
[Jerry laughs]

735
00:32:22,649 --> 00:32:25,902
Just keep putting different verbs in there
and we got another movie.

736
00:32:27,779 --> 00:32:30,531
Apes with guns and horses
and their black leather.

737
00:32:30,615 --> 00:32:31,699
And secrets.

738
00:32:31,783 --> 00:32:33,701
-"It's the forbidden zone!"
-[Jerry laughs]

739
00:32:34,243 --> 00:32:38,623
Now, have you ever heard that at lunch
on Planet of the Apes...

740
00:32:39,332 --> 00:32:40,541
All the apes would go--

741
00:32:40,625 --> 00:32:42,919
-They would go with their apes.
-Yeah, that's interesting.

742
00:32:43,002 --> 00:32:44,963
The orangutans would hang
with the orangutans.

743
00:32:45,046 --> 00:32:45,880
Yeah.

744
00:32:46,130 --> 00:32:47,840
-That makes sense.
-Totally.

745
00:32:47,924 --> 00:32:48,758
[Jerry laughs]

746
00:32:51,260 --> 00:32:54,347
Chimps have done such horrible stuff
since then.

747
00:32:54,430 --> 00:32:56,933
I'm sure they were doing it
back then also, but we didn't know.

748
00:32:57,016 --> 00:33:00,186
-Chimps ripping people's faces off.
-Oh, yeah, yeah.

749
00:33:00,645 --> 00:33:03,940
When we were kids, apes were so cool,
'cause they looked like us...

750
00:33:04,023 --> 00:33:05,817
We didn't know
they were ripping people's faces.

751
00:33:05,900 --> 00:33:08,069
-No, we didn't know about that.
-Curious George.

752
00:33:08,152 --> 00:33:11,614
Yeah, we didn't know he was curious
what your head looked like without a face.

753
00:33:11,698 --> 00:33:12,907
[both laugh]

754
00:33:12,991 --> 00:33:14,158
"I'm just curious."

755
00:33:14,242 --> 00:33:15,576
[both laugh]

756
00:33:15,994 --> 00:33:16,828
"Aah!"

757
00:33:18,955 --> 00:33:21,207
[Eddie] Yeah, Michael Jackson
had his chimps.

758
00:33:21,290 --> 00:33:23,334
When you would go to his house,
that Bubbles chimp,

759
00:33:23,418 --> 00:33:24,961
-when it got to a certain age...
-Yeah.

760
00:33:25,044 --> 00:33:27,463
...you couldn't [bleep] with Bubbles.
He had Bubbles in a cage.

761
00:33:27,547 --> 00:33:29,382
-Like, "Don't go near him."
-[Jerry laughs]

762
00:33:29,465 --> 00:33:31,884
[giggles] "Don't go over there, Eddie."

763
00:33:32,427 --> 00:33:33,761
Did you go to his house?

764
00:33:33,845 --> 00:33:35,138
Yeah, a few times.

765
00:33:35,221 --> 00:33:38,349
And the chimp would be, "Aah!"
Going crazy. "Is that Bubbles?"

766
00:33:38,433 --> 00:33:41,352
And he said,
"Yes. Don't go near the cage." [giggles]

767
00:33:41,436 --> 00:33:44,605
"Really? I thought Bubbles was..."
"Mm-mm, don't go over there."

768
00:33:44,689 --> 00:33:45,523
[both laugh]

769
00:33:49,777 --> 00:33:52,697
Who are these comics
that they have painted on the wall?

770
00:33:52,780 --> 00:33:54,407
I don't know, but I better be one of 'em.

771
00:33:57,285 --> 00:33:58,411
That looks like me.

772
00:33:58,494 --> 00:34:00,663
Like, that could be me,
it could be someone else.

773
00:34:00,747 --> 00:34:03,124
Could be. These are horrible, Eddie.

774
00:34:03,207 --> 00:34:05,334
-These are the worst caricatures...
-Which one is you?

775
00:34:05,418 --> 00:34:06,252
I don't know.

776
00:34:06,335 --> 00:34:08,796
-I don't wanna be in this thing.
-[Eddie laughs]

777
00:34:12,967 --> 00:34:14,844
You're right about the smell.

778
00:34:15,386 --> 00:34:17,346
It's really strong. [laughs]

779
00:34:17,430 --> 00:34:20,600
Well, it's that comedy club smell.
I think it's the... it's the bar,

780
00:34:20,683 --> 00:34:23,144
and whatever's happening
at a chemical level.

781
00:34:23,227 --> 00:34:25,271
And your body secretes, what is it?

782
00:34:25,354 --> 00:34:27,523
-Endorphins, pheromones, or whatever.
-[laughs]

783
00:34:27,607 --> 00:34:31,194
That shit, a group of people
doing that in the room all the time...

784
00:34:31,277 --> 00:34:32,737
-Right.
-...is gonna leave a smell.

785
00:34:32,820 --> 00:34:34,530
-For years and years.
-Years and years.

786
00:34:34,614 --> 00:34:35,907
It's different from a bar.

787
00:34:35,990 --> 00:34:37,366
It's different from a bar.

788
00:34:37,450 --> 00:34:40,703
-It's that other thing.
-It's like a bar and a living room

789
00:34:40,787 --> 00:34:42,038
soaked in beer.

790
00:34:42,121 --> 00:34:43,956
And a hint of piss, too.

791
00:34:44,040 --> 00:34:46,709
A Hint of Piss is a good name
for a comedy club.

792
00:34:48,795 --> 00:34:50,713
I remember on the weekend
I'd go to the early show

793
00:34:50,797 --> 00:34:52,298
at the East Side Comedy Club.

794
00:34:52,381 --> 00:34:56,260
Then I would get to the last spot
in the early show

795
00:34:56,469 --> 00:34:58,262
at the, uh, Rainy Nighthouse.

796
00:34:58,346 --> 00:35:01,933
Then I'd go to the Comic Strip
and do the first spot in the late show.

797
00:35:02,016 --> 00:35:05,019
-Wow.
-And then drive back out to Long Island

798
00:35:05,103 --> 00:35:08,397
and do a last spot in the late...
Do like five or six sets, you know.

799
00:35:09,607 --> 00:35:12,443
Did you ever see Pryor get up
in a little club?

800
00:35:12,652 --> 00:35:14,987
You know, I had a strange relationship
with Richard.

801
00:35:15,071 --> 00:35:17,073
Back when I broke,

802
00:35:17,824 --> 00:35:23,037
the town was still, like, doing
like a one black guy at a time thing.

803
00:35:23,412 --> 00:35:27,125
And... so, when I showed up,
Richard kind of had this...

804
00:35:27,375 --> 00:35:31,587
There was this feeling like,
"Oh, this is the new-- the new one is."

805
00:35:31,754 --> 00:35:34,549
And Richard kind of felt threatened
like, "Oh, now..."

806
00:35:34,632 --> 00:35:36,759
So, Richard would get weird. It'd be like,

807
00:35:36,843 --> 00:35:39,595
if I found out Richard was gonna be
somewhere and we all went,

808
00:35:39,679 --> 00:35:42,473
Richard would pull up and go like,
"Who's in there?"

809
00:35:42,557 --> 00:35:45,184
And he'd be like, "Oh, so Eddie Murphy's
over there." He would leave.

810
00:35:45,268 --> 00:35:46,519
-Really?
-Yeah, he'd be like,

811
00:35:46,602 --> 00:35:48,980
not want to work out in front of me.
It was strange.

812
00:35:49,063 --> 00:35:50,857
-Oh, man.
-And I just wanted to puppy dog him.

813
00:35:50,940 --> 00:35:51,983
-I was just like...
-Yeah.

814
00:35:52,066 --> 00:35:54,485
I found out all this out,
like, afterwards, that,

815
00:35:54,569 --> 00:35:55,778
"Really, he would do that?"

816
00:35:56,195 --> 00:35:59,198
He had a weird "I'm threatened"...

817
00:35:59,991 --> 00:36:01,659
Like, there's a changing of the guards,

818
00:36:01,742 --> 00:36:03,661
-or this new thing is gonna happen.
-Right, right.

819
00:36:03,744 --> 00:36:06,622
That was the weirdness with Cosby as well
in the early days.

820
00:36:06,706 --> 00:36:10,168
He thought I was this new thing,
and they felt threatened by it.

821
00:36:10,251 --> 00:36:13,171
Yes. What's so funny to me,
when I think back on it, 

822
00:36:13,254 --> 00:36:16,257
how funny it was that he thought

823
00:36:16,674 --> 00:36:21,762
he had the power to tell you to work
the way he wanted you to work.

824
00:36:22,597 --> 00:36:25,516
-What crazy fantasy was that?
-That's just ego.

825
00:36:25,600 --> 00:36:26,934
-Yeah.
-That ego.

826
00:36:27,018 --> 00:36:28,269
-[Jerry laughs]
-Yeah.

827
00:36:28,352 --> 00:36:29,687
-'Cause, see, he...
-Remember that?

828
00:36:29,770 --> 00:36:31,647
-I'm sure you remember that.
-It was his ego.

829
00:36:31,731 --> 00:36:35,109
I even in the moment thought, "Is he
out of his mind, telling him how to work?

830
00:36:35,192 --> 00:36:36,694
He's gonna work how he wants to work."

831
00:36:36,777 --> 00:36:38,029
Because he did that,

832
00:36:38,112 --> 00:36:40,531
you know, I was like...
That's one of things that makes...

833
00:36:40,615 --> 00:36:43,576
He had a weird thing with me
that he didn't have with other comics,

834
00:36:43,659 --> 00:36:45,786
'cause I've heard other people
tell stories about,

835
00:36:45,870 --> 00:36:49,457
"Oh, I had this with him, and he said
this to me. He gave me this advice."

836
00:36:49,540 --> 00:36:53,252
And the stuff that he said to me
was the exact opposite.

837
00:36:53,336 --> 00:36:55,254
And it was mean, it was like...

838
00:36:55,338 --> 00:36:56,422
I did this bit about...

839
00:36:56,505 --> 00:36:58,633
Somebody heckled me and I said,
"Shut the [bleep] up

840
00:36:58,716 --> 00:37:01,135
-before I throw my wallet over there."
-Right.

841
00:37:01,219 --> 00:37:02,178
And he called me up,

842
00:37:02,261 --> 00:37:05,681
"You can't talk about how much money
you have on stage."

843
00:37:05,765 --> 00:37:06,933
He's taking it out of context.

844
00:37:07,016 --> 00:37:09,602
He was like, "Well, you know,
I don't come to see people's show.

845
00:37:09,685 --> 00:37:13,022
And I know you like Richard." 
And I was like, "Yeah..."

846
00:37:13,105 --> 00:37:16,067
Then he told me,
"I'm in Atlantic City this weekend.

847
00:37:16,150 --> 00:37:19,070
If you-- You should come and see
how it's supposed to be done.

848
00:37:19,153 --> 00:37:20,863
-Oh, man.
-You shouldn't get on the stage

849
00:37:20,947 --> 00:37:22,323
unless you have something to say."

850
00:37:22,657 --> 00:37:24,825
-That's what he told me. I was like...
-Wow.

851
00:37:26,911 --> 00:37:28,204
He wasn't nice.

852
00:37:28,287 --> 00:37:31,457
He wasn't doing that with everybody.
He did that with me, specifically.

853
00:37:31,540 --> 00:37:33,417
-Right.
-He was shitty with me.

854
00:37:35,836 --> 00:37:37,004
[Eddie] Is that a homeless guy

855
00:37:37,088 --> 00:37:39,298
-sitting in front of the Moustache Cafe?
-Yeah.

856
00:37:39,882 --> 00:37:41,467
[Eddie] Wow. You got a lot of--

857
00:37:41,550 --> 00:37:44,011
You know who homeless people
must have contempt for?

858
00:37:44,095 --> 00:37:44,929
Campers.

859
00:37:45,012 --> 00:37:46,055
[Jerry laughs]

860
00:37:46,138 --> 00:37:48,599
It'd be like, "So, this is...
it's a [bleep] joke to y'all.

861
00:37:48,683 --> 00:37:50,810
-[Jerry laughs]
-You just playing around. I'm out here."

862
00:37:50,893 --> 00:37:52,812
"Oh, so you think it's fun
to hang outside."

863
00:37:52,895 --> 00:37:55,815
"You're playing around, huh?
In your little tent, [bleep].

864
00:37:55,898 --> 00:37:57,733
-I'm out here for real."
-[Jerry laughs]

865
00:37:57,817 --> 00:37:59,694
-Can you do that bit?
-Absolutely.

866
00:37:59,777 --> 00:38:01,112
[laughs] All right.

867
00:38:01,195 --> 00:38:04,615
You can do that bit, and I'd get a kick
out of seeing you do that and being like,

868
00:38:04,699 --> 00:38:06,242
"I gave him that bit."

869
00:38:06,325 --> 00:38:07,827
I'd get such a kick out of it.

870
00:38:11,455 --> 00:38:14,125
When I used to finish my sets
at the Comic Strip

871
00:38:14,208 --> 00:38:17,586
and I would drive back out to Massapequa,
I'd put disco on.

872
00:38:17,670 --> 00:38:18,629
You like disco?

873
00:38:18,713 --> 00:38:19,714
I love disco.

874
00:38:19,797 --> 00:38:22,049
It was always so positive and upbeat.

875
00:38:22,133 --> 00:38:23,092
Don't rock the boat.

876
00:38:23,175 --> 00:38:25,303
-[laughing] Don't rock the boat, baby.
-[laughs]

877
00:38:26,929 --> 00:38:29,015
There was nothing like those sets, right?

878
00:38:29,265 --> 00:38:30,558
-When it would go well?
-Nothing.

879
00:38:30,641 --> 00:38:33,269
And then, three or four comics
would go to a diner...

880
00:38:33,477 --> 00:38:34,312
-afterwards.
-Yeah.

881
00:38:34,395 --> 00:38:35,521
And then, it's just...

882
00:38:35,604 --> 00:38:37,565
-You laugh till your throat hurts.
-Yeah.

883
00:38:37,898 --> 00:38:39,775
And I realize it was fun then, too.

884
00:38:39,859 --> 00:38:41,986
It was not like, "Oh, yeah,
I didn't realize I was..."

885
00:38:42,069 --> 00:38:44,572
Like, I realize
I was having a ball that night.

886
00:38:44,655 --> 00:38:45,614
Right.

887
00:38:47,992 --> 00:38:50,119
-'Cause once you've made somebody laugh...
-Yeah.

888
00:38:50,202 --> 00:38:51,746
-...you've got 'em forever.
-Right.

889
00:38:51,829 --> 00:38:53,873
-If you really made them laugh...
-Yeah.

890
00:38:53,956 --> 00:38:55,333
...they always think you're funny.

891
00:38:55,416 --> 00:38:57,668
So, why is it so... still so hard?

892
00:38:58,127 --> 00:38:59,920
-I don't find--
-That's in your mind.

893
00:39:00,004 --> 00:39:01,464
-It is in my mind.
-It's in your mind.

894
00:39:01,547 --> 00:39:03,966
-It didn't feel that easy to me.
-It's hard because you--

895
00:39:04,050 --> 00:39:05,426
You want to be good,

896
00:39:05,509 --> 00:39:06,844
that's why you're a great comic.

897
00:39:06,927 --> 00:39:10,473
'Cause you always wanna [bleep] be good,
and you want it to be better.

898
00:39:10,556 --> 00:39:11,807
-And you wanna get...
-Right.

899
00:39:11,891 --> 00:39:15,227
So, that's in your mind,
where it's like, "Oh, the pressure and..."

900
00:39:15,519 --> 00:39:17,772
But the audience is just happy to see you.

901
00:39:19,815 --> 00:39:22,902
And I stopped doing it. That was one
of things that was shitty, me being

902
00:39:22,985 --> 00:39:24,028
on stage and...

903
00:39:24,111 --> 00:39:26,238
I think I might have even said like....

904
00:39:26,489 --> 00:39:29,408
"I just could just stand here
and you [bleep] guys would laugh it in."

905
00:39:29,492 --> 00:39:32,495
And I remember I had times
where I just... I just stood there

906
00:39:32,578 --> 00:39:35,539
-for, you know, ten minutes, and just...
-Right.

907
00:39:35,623 --> 00:39:36,499
And they would laugh.

908
00:39:36,582 --> 00:39:39,043
And you just stand there,
and they'd giggle again.

909
00:39:39,126 --> 00:39:42,088
And then you'd make, like, a little face,
like, "Yah!"

910
00:39:42,630 --> 00:39:45,591
And you literally could...
you literally could just stand there.

911
00:39:46,175 --> 00:39:48,469
For the ones that really, really came out 
to see you.

912
00:39:48,552 --> 00:39:49,637
Yeah. Right, right.

913
00:39:49,720 --> 00:39:53,974
There's no luckier person in show business
than the comedian.

914
00:39:54,475 --> 00:39:55,810
You can make a living...

915
00:39:56,769 --> 00:39:58,020
making people laugh.

916
00:39:58,104 --> 00:39:59,105
I agree.

917
00:39:59,271 --> 00:40:02,274
That's why I don't want any awards for it.

918
00:40:06,904 --> 00:40:08,864
How many kids you got
living in this house?

919
00:40:08,948 --> 00:40:09,990
-Three.
-Three.

920
00:40:10,491 --> 00:40:11,534
That's nice.

921
00:40:11,617 --> 00:40:12,993
Getting ready to be four.

922
00:40:13,536 --> 00:40:15,663
-My other kids are grown-ups.
-Right.

923
00:40:15,746 --> 00:40:16,622
In their 20s.

924
00:40:17,039 --> 00:40:19,792
But is there anything better
than coming home and the kids are home?

925
00:40:19,875 --> 00:40:20,960
-Nothing.
-Nothing.

926
00:40:21,043 --> 00:40:23,712
But now, it's like,
 the older kids have moved out.

927
00:40:23,796 --> 00:40:25,798
But my house is like, uh...

928
00:40:27,174 --> 00:40:29,677
the center of it all.
Everybody comes to the house.

929
00:40:29,760 --> 00:40:31,429
-Right.
-They're always at the house.

930
00:40:31,512 --> 00:40:32,930
-That's nice.
-Yeah, I love it.

931
00:40:33,013 --> 00:40:34,473
And is that a Sunday thing?

932
00:40:34,557 --> 00:40:35,724
Just... all the time.

933
00:40:35,808 --> 00:40:37,685
-All the time?
-Yeah, my house is the...

934
00:40:37,768 --> 00:40:41,522
The center of, you know,
our little world, is my house.

935
00:40:41,605 --> 00:40:42,773
-It's the greatest.
-Yeah.

936
00:40:42,857 --> 00:40:45,734
But once they're 15 or 16,
their social life takes off.

937
00:40:45,818 --> 00:40:47,236
They're moving around, socializing.

938
00:40:47,319 --> 00:40:49,488
-But when they move out, it's different.
-Oh, yeah?

939
00:40:49,572 --> 00:40:51,449
Yeah, it's one thing
when they go and socialize

940
00:40:51,532 --> 00:40:53,117
-and they always come back to you.
-Right.

941
00:40:53,200 --> 00:40:55,119
-When they move out for real...
-Yeah.

942
00:40:55,202 --> 00:40:58,998
...you just cry like...
Oh, you cry, cry like an old grandmother.

943
00:40:59,081 --> 00:41:03,252
When they leave, you go in the room
and [bleep] fall apart.

944
00:41:06,714 --> 00:41:08,215
Hey, did you get that, uh...

945
00:41:09,425 --> 00:41:11,260
-Mark Twain Award?
-No.

946
00:41:13,554 --> 00:41:16,015
-You should get the Mark Twain--
